const answers = [
{ id:"Q001", answer:"After taking Oathkeeper during Act, the Exile’s next Wake checks Usurper victory before the title flips, so they are not yet Usurper and cannot win then. If they still hold the title, it flips to Usurper after that check; they can first win at the following Wake, or at the end of round 8 if that comes first while they still hold it as Usurper. Losing the title prevents the win; retaking it resets it to Oathkeeper and restarts the sequence.", support:"supported", evidence:"Title transfer and victory timing: “next Wake check (not yet Usurper), then flip to Usurper, then win at the following Wake or at the end of round 8”." },
{ id:"Q002", answer:"Oath of Supremacy matches Vision of Conquest: rule the most sites. Oath of the People matches Vision of Rebellion: hold People’s Favor. Oath of Protection matches Vision of Sanctuary: hold the most relics and banners combined. Oath of Devotion matches Vision of Faith: hold Darkest Secret. For a Visionary win, an Exile must have a true Vision revealed, complete its goal at Wake, and the whole game must have drawn at least 3 Visions from the world deck. For Conquest and Sanctuary, “most” means strictly more than every other player; ties do not count.", support:"supported", evidence:"Oathkeeper title and four Oaths; Visionary victory: “at least 3 Visions drawn from world deck by all players combined” and “strictly more than every other player”." },
{ id:"Q003", answer:"At the ends of rounds 5, 6, and 7, roll the end die only if the Chancellor or a Citizen is Oathkeeper. The game ends on 6 in round 5, 5–6 in round 6, or 3–6 in round 7. Chancellor wins unless a Citizen meeting the Successor goal wins alone instead. An Exile Oathkeeper or Usurper prevents the roll.", support:"supported", evidence:"Stable regime: “at ends of rounds 5, 6 and 7, roll end die only if Chancellor or Citizen is Oathkeeper”." },
{ id:"Q004", answer:"At the end of round 8, check in order: (1) if Chancellor or Citizen is Oathkeeper, Empire wins (Chancellor unless a qualifying Citizen Successor wins alone); (2) otherwise an Exile Usurper wins; (3) otherwise a qualifying revealed Exile Vision wins, with simultaneous qualifiers ordered Conquest, Rebellion, Sanctuary, Faith; (4) otherwise Empire wins, including when an Exile is only Oathkeeper, again with a qualifying Successor replacing the Chancellor. A Citizen’s Successor condition is checked whenever the Chancellor would win; meeting it alone does not end the game.", support:"supported", evidence:"War exhaustion: four numbered priority rules; “If several qualify, priority Conquest → Rebellion → Sanctuary → Faith.”" },
{ id:"Q005", answer:"Citizen Successor goals by current Oath: Supremacy—hold more relics and banners combined than the Chancellor and every other Citizen; People—hold Darkest Secret; Protection—hold People’s Favor; Devotion—hold Grand Scepter. A Citizen cannot win by a revealed Vision or by becoming Usurper; Citizens do not use Vision or Usurper victory.", support:"supported", evidence:"Citizen succession table; “Citizens/Chancellor cannot win by true Visions or Usurper.”" },
{ id:"Q006", answer:"At Wake, if you hold People’s Favor, choose to put 1 favor from your board onto it or return 1 from it to the least-full suit bank, choosing among tied banks. Never remove its last favor: with exactly 1, add if able; if you have none, do nothing. If it is Mob-side, repeat the choice once. Only after those choices, flip it to Mob if it has 6 or more favor. Becoming Mob at that final check does not cause another payment that Wake.", support:"supported", evidence:"Wake step 1: “Never remove its last favor”; Mob-side “repeat this choice once”; “Afterwards, if it has 6+ favor, flip to Mob.”" },
{ id:"Q007", answer:"Rest in order: return favor on denizens/edifices to matching suit banks; return secrets on denizens/edifices/relics to your board and flip facedown secrets on your board faceup; refresh Supply; save unspent Supply; then optionally use each Rest power once, in chosen order. Chancellor refreshes by warbands in personal bank: 18+ = 6 Supply, 11–17 = 5, 4–10 = 4, 0–3 = 3. Exile: 9+ = 6, 4–8 = 5, 0–3 = 4. Citizen refreshes to the exact Chancellor marker space, including leftmost. For each unspent Supply, move the marker one extra space left, capped at the leftmost space (7 Supply).", support:"supported", evidence:"Rest: mandatory order, steps 1–5." },
{ id:"Q008", answer:"Search costs 2–4 Supply and chooses either the world deck or the discard pile for your pawn’s region, never both. Discard-pile Search costs 2. World-deck Search costs 2 with zero Visions drawn, 3 with 1–2, and 4 with 3–5; Darkest Secret replaces that cost with 2, while other applicable modifiers still apply. Draw up to 3, stopping early only if the source runs out, a world-deck Vision stops you, or a modifier reduces the draw. From the world deck draw one at a time; if a Vision appears, announce that a Vision was drawn, stop immediately, and advance Visions Drawn by 1. A Vision drawn from a discard pile neither stops drawing nor advances the track. Discard all but one drawn card before playing it, or discard the last one too.", support:"supported", evidence:"Search section: sources/costs and Vision distinction." },
{ id:"Q009", answer:"Discarded cards go facedown atop the next region’s pile in the cycle Cradle → Provinces → Hinterland → Cradle; order multiple discards as you choose, and never use the same-region pile. For cards in hand or advisers, choose the destination based on your pawn’s region. For discarding a card at a site in another region, use the cycle based on that card’s region, not your pawn’s. Favor on a discarded card immediately returns to its matching bank; secrets on it go to the acting player’s board facedown.", support:"supported", evidence:"Discard destinations paragraph." },
{ id:"Q010", answer:"You may have up to 3 advisers total, faceup and facedown. Before exceeding the limit, discard an adviser subject to locks; if the incoming card changes the adviser limit, check against the new limit (use the lowest of multiple limits). A facedown adviser has no suit, restriction banner, or power; any card, including a site-only card or Vision, may be held facedown. Later, a facedown adviser may be played faceup legally or discarded as a minor action; a faceup adviser cannot be discarded at will and leaves only when a rule permits or requires it. A true Vision may be held facedown, but only an Exile can play/reveal it to the separate Revealed Vision space; that replaces and discards the Exile’s old revealed Vision and does not use an adviser slot. Chancellor/Citizens cannot reveal true Visions.", support:"supported", evidence:"Search—Adviser and True Vision options; Minor actions." },
{ id:"Q011", answer:"Muster (1 Supply): at your pawn’s site, place 1 favor on an empty denizen or intact edifice (no favor or secrets already), then gain 2 warbands from your personal bank to your board; Citizens gain purple. Relics and ruins are ineligible; you need not rule the site, but cannot Muster remotely. Trade (1 Supply) also uses an empty denizen/intact edifice at your pawn’s site. Put 1 secret on it to gain 1 favor plus 1 per matching faceup adviser from that card’s suit bank; or put 2 favor on it to gain 1 secret from the shared bank per matching faceup adviser. Facedown advisers do not match; if none match, the second trade gains zero secrets, but the cost is still paid. Finite sources give only what is available; secrets are unlimited.", support:"supported", evidence:"Muster and Trade sections." },
{ id:"Q012", answer:"Recover costs 1 Supply plus the target cost. You may recover a facedown relic at your site by paying its site cost (3 favor to the indicated bank, burn 2 favor, burn 1 secret, or burn 2 secrets), then hold it faceup in your personal bank. Or recover either banner from anywhere, including yourself or the shared bank. For People’s Favor, pay strictly more favor than it currently has; put the payment on it, distribute its old favor one at a time through suit banks starting at any chosen bank and moving away from the world deck in printed bank order, wrapping as needed, then flip it to normal if Mob. For Darkest Secret, pay strictly more secrets than it has; put those on it, take 1 old secret, and the previous holder takes the rest. If recovering it from yourself, all old secrets return to you; if from shared bank, the remainder stays there. Recovering a banner has no seizure penalty.", support:"supported", evidence:"Recover section, People’s Favor and Darkest Secret bullets." },
{ id:"Q013", answer:"To recover another player’s Darkest Secret, their pawn’s site must have at least one suited card whose suit matches none of that player’s faceup advisers. One mismatching card is enough even if their other site cards match. A ruin has no suit and facedown advisers have no suit. If the holder has no faceup advisers but a suited card is at the site, it is recoverable. Empty sites and ruin-only sites protect it because they have no suited card. Recovering from yourself or the shared bank ignores this protection.", support:"supported", evidence:"Darkest Secret protection, corrected by official errata." },
{ id:"Q014", answer:"Choose any other player who rules your site or whose pawn is there; if no player rules your site, you may choose bandits. At least one target must be at your pawn’s site, and if the defender rules that site you must target it. You may also target any of the defender’s sites anywhere on the map (each adds 1 defense die). If the defender’s pawn is at your site, you may target any of their relics (printed defense dice), banners (dice equal to resources on the banner), or their pawn and board favor together (2 dice). A relic of a co-located defender satisfies the local-target requirement.", support:"supported", evidence:"Campaign steps 1–2: Choose defender; Declare targets." },
{ id:"Q015", answer:"The attacker chooses up to as many attack dice as warbands in their attacking force. Defender rolls defense dice: count shields, doubling shields for each double-symbol (multipliers stack), then add all defending warbands at targeted sites, the defender’s board force if their pawn is at the attacker’s site or any targeted site, and 1 per targeted bandit site. Doubles multiply shields only, not warbands. On attack, each skull immediately kills one of the attacker’s own force warbands; count full swords, each pair of hollow swords as one, and a leftover hollow sword as zero. The attacker may sacrifice surviving force warbands for +1 attack each, but if sacrificing must sacrifice exactly enough to win, never excess. Attack must strictly exceed defense; a tie or lower is a defender win.", support:"supported", evidence:"Campaign steps 4–5." },
{ id:"Q016", answer:"On attacker victory, first the defender kills half the warbands in its defending force, rounded down, returning the rest to its board; resolve defeated battle-plan effects. Then the attacker may place any number, including zero, of surviving force warbands on any targeted sites. Zero leaves a site to bandits unless another power applies. The attacker takes all targeted relics and banners. A seized banner (taken any way except Recover) loses 2 resources, to a minimum of 1; People’s Favor flips to Mob. If pawn and favor were targeted, the attacker may force the defender to travel to a legal chosen site for no Supply and may independently burn half the defender’s board favor, rounded down. Respect travel restrictions; if Shrouded Wood overrides the choice, its enemy ruler chooses the destination (Chancellor chooses for Empire). Then resolve attacker’s victorious battle-plan effects; finally discard plans marked “At end, discard.”", support:"supported", evidence:"Campaign steps 6–8." },
{ id:"Q017", answer:"When an Imperial player defends, the Chancellor automatically joins as Ally. Other non-attacking Citizens may join with the defender’s permission if their pawn is at the attacker’s site or a targeted site. Activate relevant modifiers ruled by defender/Allies; the defending side shares powers as one side, though “your site” and “your board” still refer to each user’s own site/board. Allied board warbands add defense only if their pawn is at the attacker’s site or a targeted site. Defenders/Allies may use plans at Imperial sites and their own advisers, not another player’s advisers; each shared plan can be used once total by one user who pays. The declared defender chooses order, timing, and priority if Allies disagree. Chancellor chooses Imperial defending casualties; surviving site warbands go to Chancellor’s board while surviving Citizen board warbands stay there. A Citizen attacking Chancellor or another Citizen is non-Imperial for that Campaign; if Chancellor attacks a Citizen, that Citizen is non-Imperial. An intra-Empire Campaign does not make all Citizens non-Imperial.", support:"supported", evidence:"Campaign: Imperial defense; Enemies paragraph." },
{ id:"Q018", answer:"Base Travel cost by origin-to-destination region (Cradle / Provinces / Hinterland columns): from Cradle 1 / 2 / 4; from Provinces 2 / 2 / 2; from Hinterland 4 / 2 / 3. Travel is to any different site, not just an adjacent one. Reveal a facedown destination, put its printed favor/secrets there from the shared bank, and put its printed number of relics beside it facedown from the relic deck. Pay the fixed-number cost first, then apply other increases/decreases. Free Travel is the final override: an applicable “spend no Supply” power makes Supply paid zero despite an increase. Site-entry restrictions still apply unless a power ignores them.", support:"supported", evidence:"Travel section and cost table." },
{ id:"Q019", answer:"The Grand Scepter holder cannot use its powers on the turn they took it. On a later turn, its holder—even an Exile—may offer Citizenship to any Exile, including themself; an offer must promise exactly one Reliquary relic. They may additionally negotiate binding exchanges of favor, secrets, banners, and non-Reliquary relics, and may let the recipient peek at Reliquary relics. Other binding-deal powers cannot trade Reliquary relics. On acceptance, the Exile flips to Citizen and replaces all their board/map warbands with purple (if purple is short, they choose what to remove and replace), discards their revealed Vision, flips Usurper to Oathkeeper, ends their Act if accepting on their own turn, and refreshes to full Supply. Exchange promised pieces; Chancellor gains the newly uncovered mandatory Reliquary modifier. Forced Citizenship uses the same conversion without a negotiated offer, required relic gift, or invented exchange; only actual removal of a Reliquary relic uncovers its modifier.", support:"supported", evidence:"Citizenship and exile during play: Grand Scepter, Offer, Acceptance, Forced Citizenship." },
{ id:"Q020", answer:"For the next Oath, a victory by Vision alone means use the Oath matching that Vision. For any other win or multiple victory methods, choose any Oath except the current one. If an Exile wins the Chronicle, first flip all Citizen boards, including boxed boards, to Exile; the winner may then offer Chronicle Citizenship to other Exiles except those just flipped. Each accepting Exile must flip to Citizen and replace every garrison at each site they rule with exactly one warband of the winner’s color at that site. To rebuild the world deck, shuffle all denizens still in play (excluding denizens at retained sites, in Archive, or Dispossessed); make a top stack of 10 denizens plus 2 random Visions (12 cards), a middle stack of 15 denizens plus the remaining 3 Visions (18 cards), and put remaining denizens below. The aid’s “12 denizens + 2” is incorrect; the top stack is 10 + 2, not 14.", support:"supported", evidence:"Writing the Chronicle steps 1–2 and 9." }
];
